<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rionegro</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rionegro is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Antioquia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a>, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medellin">Medellín</a>. In 1863 a convention of the victorious Liberals met there and adopted the Rionegro Constitution, which created the federal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> of Colombia, with sovereign states and broad individual freedoms. It is now the site of Medellín's international airport.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rionegro.jpg" alt="Rionegro" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Cristian Santiago Gomez Zapata · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rionegro-Iglesia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The territory was first mentioned in 1541 by the Spanish lieutenant Álvaro de Mendoza, and Jorge Robledo is said to have been the first Iberian to reach the Valley of San Nicolás on 2 September 1541. On 8 November 1581 Juan Daza took possession of the land by nailing his sword in the ground, in accordance with the rituals of the laws of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">Indias</a>, and began building a farm. Settlers came from many parts of the country, among them the provincial capital Santa Fe de Antioquia, and the farm grew into a village and then a town in the first half of the 17th century. The chapel of San Nicolás el Magno was built as early as 1642.</p><p>For most of the 18th century Rionegro was isolated by jungle and mountains. By 1783 it had enough population to be treated as a municipality, and in 1786 it became the City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arma-people">Arma</a> de Rionegro under a certificate of King Carlos III. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popayan">Popayán</a> visited in 1793, found the chapel in a poor state and ordered a new one, which was inaugurated in 1803. In the 19th century Rionegro was an important centre at the end of Spanish rule and in the early years of independence, and it rivalled Santa Fe de Antioquia for the capital of Antioquia until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medellin">Medellín</a> grew far beyond both. The 1863 constitution was written in the city, which is sometimes called the Cradle of Democracy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Rionegro is a city and municipality in the subregion of Eastern Antioquia, in the central range of the Andes, at an average elevation of 2,125 metres. It covers 196 square kilometres and takes its name, meaning Black River, from a river that looks black because of the shadows the trees cast on it. The population was 101,046 at the 2005 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. It is a trading centre for the region, with industry and flower growing, and José María Córdova International Airport, which serves <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medellin">Medellín</a>, stands within it. Its historic sites include the Concatedral de San Nicolás el Magno and the Casa de la Convención.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rionegro” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Republic of Colombia was created by the constitution of 1886, which replaced the Liberal federation of the United States of Colombia set up by the <b>Rionegro</b> constitution of 1863.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-colombia">Republic of Colombia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After winning the civil war of 1860 to 1862 against the Granadine Confederation, Tomás Cipriano de Mosquera and the Liberals met at <b>Rionegro</b> in Antioquia and adopted a new constitution in 1863, creating the United States of Colombia, made up of nine sovereign states including Panama.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-states-of-colombia">United States of Colombia</a></li></ul></section>
